From cfd9bf56f6970dfd996cbcc3c9d73e4dae22154a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 02:11:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] AudioEngine: dialogue normalisation of passed-through AC-3,
 E-AC-3 and DTS at 0 dB, by a setting

A receiver lowers the sound by as much as the dialogue normalisation of the
stream says (an AC-3 stream whose dialogue is at -27 dBFS plays 4 dB lower).
People with a receiver asked to have the sound as mastered and set the
volume themselves.
The setting audiooutput.hadesdialnorm (System > Audio, passthrough group,
off by default) makes the field say 0 dB of attenuation in the frames that
pass through; nothing else of the sound changes. It departs from what the
stream asks for, so it is off by default and its help says so.

DialnormOverride.h does it in the forms that have a public specification:
- AC-3: dialnorm (and dialnorm2 of 1+1) to code 31; crc1 computed again for
  the first 5/8 of the syncframe (ATSC A/52 5.4.1.2, 7.10.1);
- E-AC-3: dialnorm (and dialnorm2) of every substream, independent and
  dependent; crc2 computed again, never equal to the syncword (crcrsv, as
  FFmpeg's encoder does);
- DTS core with VERNUM 7: DIALNORM to 0, which no CRC protects (ETSI TS 102
  114 5.4.2: the header CRC test "shall not be applied"; a frame with one is
  still left alone). Nothing of DTS-HD's extension substream is touched.
Left as they are, and the log says why: E-AC-3 with Dolby Atmos (the
extension type A flag of its additional bit stream information, object audio
metadata, or the demuxer's Atmos profile), TrueHD (nothing public describes
its dialogue norm), DTS with VERNUM 6 (its field cannot say 0 dB), with a
Rev2 auxiliary chunk that carries the dialogue norm (it has its own CRC), and
DTS in 14-bit or little-endian words. A syncframe that fails its own CRC is
never touched, and every rewritten frame is checked again (CRCs as a decoder
reads them, nothing else changed); one that does not check gets its bytes
back.

The setting is read for every frame, so it applies at once. kodi.log says,
once per stream and value, what was sent, in the unit of each: "AC-3 passed
through: dialogue level -27 dBFS (the receiver lowers the sound by 4 dB), sent
as -31 dBFS (no attenuation)", "DTS passed through: dialogue normalisation gain
-10 dB, sent as 0 dB", or why nothing was; and at the end how many frames were
rewritten.

Tested on the host with FATE's samples through Kodi's CAEStreamParser:
FFmpeg's decoders with CRC checks (crccheck+explode) say the same of the
rewritten streams as of the originals, the decoded sound is identical bit for
bit, and with dialnorm applied by FFmpeg (-target_level -31) the rewritten
stream gives the original sound as it is. DTS core, ES, XCH, XXCH, X96, XBR,
XLL (DTS-HD MA) and DTS:X: FATE's samples say DIALNORM 0, so copies that say
10 were rewritten, and gave the originals back byte for byte. FATE's E-AC-3
7.1 sample is Atmos: left as it is. Not verified with a receiver.

Idea: the dialnorm options of pannal's CoreELEC builds (p3i) and of
SamuriHL's; written anew.
